{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018r3CyW2EbK4b7ZYrVqQi5M","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\t# ── Machine-readable fields (parsed by the workflow) ──────────────────────────\n3\tapp: pinewood-derby-sim\n4\tarchetype: desktop-app\n5\trepo: ./\n6\tdescription: An interactive simulator that teaches kids the physics of building a fast pinewood derby car.\n7\t\n8\tlanguage: [python]\n9\tframework: []                        # engine is dependency-free; pygame is the likely UI layer (deferred)\n10\truntime: \"python 3.12\"\n11\tpackage_manager: uv                  # uv-managed venv + Python 3.12\n12\t\n13\tcommands:                            # exact commands agents run (the contract for tooling)\n14\t  install: \"uv sync\"\n15\t  test: \"uv run pytest\"              # ← QA writes to this; developer runs it to reach GREEN\n16\t  test_one: \"uv run pytest {path}\"\n17\t  lint: \"uv run ruff check .\"\n18\t  typecheck: \"uv run mypy .\"\n19\t  build: \"\"                          # N/A for a desktop app\n20\t  run: \"uv run python -m pinewood_derby\"\n21\t\n22\tquality_bars:\n23\t  coverage: \">= 90% on the simulation engine (pure logic); >= 70% overall\"\n24\t  performance: \"a single race simulation computes in < 5ms\"\n25\t  security: \"no PII collected; no network calls; safe for children\"\n26\t\n27\tproduct_plane:\n28\t  skills: ./.claude/skills           # app-specific skills (override system plane)\n29\t  hooks: ./.claude/settings.json     # app-specific hooks\n30\t  agents: []                         # rare app/archetype specialist agents\n31\t\n32\tarchetype_plane: []                  # none yet; desktop-app-plane artifacts will emerge from the loop\n33\t---\n34\t\n35\t# Pinewood Derby Simulator — Profile\n36\t\n37\t> Teaches kids the *best way* to build a pinewood derby car by letting them adjust a car's design\n38\t> and watch the physics play out on a simulated track. The learning goal is as important as the\n39\t> simulation: every result should reinforce a real, correct building principle.\n40\t\n41\t## Overview\n42\tA simulator where a child configures a pinewood derby car — weight and where it's placed, axle and\n43\twheel prep, alignment, lubrication, body shape — and races it on a standard sloped track. The sim\n44\tshows the run, the time, and *why* the car performed as it did, building intuition for the\n45\treal-world principles. Audience: kids ~7–12 (and their parents/leaders), so the UI must be simple,\n46\tvisual, and encouraging.\n47\t\n48\t## Domain & glossary\n49\tThe simulation must be **physically grounded** — teaching wrong physics is the worst possible bug.\n50\tCore established principles to model:\n51\t\n52\t- **Weight limit** — Standard max is **5.0 oz (141.7 g)**. Build up to, not over, the limit. More\n53\t  mass = more potential energy on the slope.\n54\t- **Weight placement** — Mass toward the **rear** (commonly ~1 inch ahead of the rear axle) sits\n55\t  higher on the slope longer, converting more potential energy to kinetic energy. Too far back =\n56\t  unstable / can pop a wheel; this is a real tradeoff to model.\n57\t- **Friction (the biggest lever after weight)** — Polished axles, removed burrs/crimp marks, and\n58\t  dry graphite lubrication reduce rolling and bore friction. Less wheel-to-body and\n59\t  wheel-to-axle-head contact = faster.\n60\t- **Alignment** — A car that drifts into the rails or wanders loses energy. \"Rail-riding\" (a tiny\n61\t  consistent steer) and reducing the number of wheels in contact reduce friction losses.\n62\t- **Wheels** — Lighter, true (round, balanced) wheels with smooth bores have lower rotational\n63\t  inertia and friction.\n64\t- **Aerodynamics** — Minor at derby speeds but non-zero; lower, smoother bodies help slightly. Keep\n65\t  this appropriately small so kids learn the *real* priority order (weight & friction first).\n66\t\n67\t> **Glossary**\n68\t> - **PE/KE** — potential / kinetic energy; the core of why placement and weight matter.\n69\t> - **Rail-riding** — deliberately steering a car to glide along one rail consistently.\n70\t> - **DNF** — a car that derails or stalls; the sim should make failure modes legible.\n71\t\n72\t## Conventions\n73\tRules the **developer** follows and the **auditor** checks:\n74\t\n75\t- **Architecture:** strict separation of a **pure simulation engine** (`pinewood_derby` package, no\n76\t  UI / no I/O) from any UI layer (pygame, deferred). The engine is where the physics lives and\n77\t  where TDD is richest.\n78\t- **Engine style:** pure functions / immutable dataclasses, no hidden state, deterministic given\n79\t  inputs (a fixed seed for any randomness) so tests are reproducible.\n80\t- **Typing:** full type hints; **mypy `strict`** must pass. Model domain quantities explicitly\n81\t  (don't pass bare floats where grams vs ounces could be confused — use named types/units).\n82\t- **Testing:** `pytest`, behavior-driven against the engine's physics (e.g. \"more rear weight,\n83\t  within the stable range, yields a faster time\"); test principles and tradeoffs, not\n84\t  implementation details.\n85\t- **Lint:** `ruff` clean.\n86\t- **Dependencies:** keep the engine **dependency-free**; UI deps (pygame) may be added when the UI\n87\t  is built.\n88\t\n89\t## Constraints\n90\t- **Educational accuracy is a hard requirement** — simulated outcomes must reflect real derby\n91\t  physics and the correct priority order of techniques. A \"fun but wrong\" result is a bug.\n92\t- **Rich physics, simple presentation** — the simulation stays physically deep, but nothing\n93\t  technical (Joules, Newtons, coefficients, integration) ever reaches the kid. Anything *shown* to a\n94\t  ~12-year-old (or a group/demo) must be plain-language, visual, and immediately legible: \"where did\n95\t  your speed go?\", not an energy ledger. Complexity lives behind the engine boundary; the\n96\t  presentation layer translates it. A result a kid can't make sense of is a usability bug.\n97\t- **No network, no accounts, no data collection** — safe for children; runs locally/offline.\n98\t- **Deployment target:** local desktop (pygame) for v1; a browser front-end may be revisited later\n99\t  (the pure engine ports either way).\n100\t- **Accessibility:** age-appropriate, readable, keyboard-navigable UI when built.\n101\t\n102\t## Out of scope / known limitations\n103\t- No real-hardware integration, no multiplayer, no user accounts/leaderboards (for now).\n104\t- Not a CAD/3D modeling tool — it teaches principles, it doesn't design a physical cut file.\n105\t- Aerodynamics is modeled at low fidelity by design.\n106\t\n107\t## Product-plane notes\n108\t_(empty — the synthesizer will populate app-specific skills/hooks/learnings here as it discovers\n109\tthem, e.g. a \"physics-validation\" skill if engine accuracy becomes a recurring friction point.)_\n110\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"55f4d0f9-3f78-404c-9d9b-b1c51c035e71","uuid":"4fd0a315-461f-4e7b-87a9-a4be58333e51","timestamp":"2026-06-03T03:44:52.226Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/ericburdett/Projects/agentic-sdlc/apps/pinewood-derby-sim/profile.md","content":"---\n# ── Machine-readable fields (parsed by the workflow) ──────────────────────────\napp: pinewood-derby-sim\narchetype: desktop-app\nrepo: ./\ndescription: An interactive simulator that teaches kids the physics of building a fast pinewood derby car.\n\nlanguage: [python]\nframework: []                        # engine is dependency-free; pygame is the likely UI layer (deferred)\nruntime: \"python 3.12\"\npackage_manager: uv                  # uv-managed venv + Python 3.12\n\ncommands:                            # exact commands agents run (the contract for tooling)\n  install: \"uv sync\"\n  test: \"uv run pytest\"              # ← QA writes to this; developer runs it to reach GREEN\n  test_one: \"uv run pytest {path}\"\n  lint: \"uv run ruff check .\"\n  typecheck: \"uv run mypy .\"\n  build: \"\"                          # N/A for a desktop app\n  run: \"uv run python -m pinewood_derby\"\n\nquality_bars:\n  coverage: \">= 90% on the simulation engine (pure logic); >= 70% overall\"\n  performance: \"a single race simulation computes in < 5ms\"\n  security: \"no PII collected; no network calls; safe for children\"\n\nproduct_plane:\n  skills: ./.claude/skills           # app-specific skills (override system plane)\n  hooks: ./.claude/settings.json     # app-specific hooks\n  agents: []                         # rare app/archetype specialist agents\n\narchetype_plane: []                  # none yet; desktop-app-plane artifacts will emerge from the loop\n---\n\n# Pinewood Derby Simulator — Profile\n\n> Teaches kids the *best way* to build a pinewood derby car by letting them adjust a car's design\n> and watch the physics play out on a simulated track. The learning goal is as important as the\n> simulation: every result should reinforce a real, correct building principle.\n\n## Overview\nA simulator where a child configures a pinewood derby car — weight and where it's placed, axle and\nwheel prep, alignment, lubrication, body shape — and races it on a standard sloped track. The sim\nshows the run, the time, and *why* the car performed as it did, building intuition for the\nreal-world principles. Audience: kids ~7–12 (and their parents/leaders), so the UI must be simple,\nvisual, and encouraging.\n\n## Domain & glossary\nThe simulation must be **physically grounded** — teaching wrong physics is the worst possible bug.\nCore established principles to model:\n\n- **Weight limit** — Standard max is **5.0 oz (141.7 g)**. Build up to, not over, the limit. More\n  mass = more potential energy on the slope.\n- **Weight placement** — Mass toward the **rear** (commonly ~1 inch ahead of the rear axle) sits\n  higher on the slope longer, converting more potential energy to kinetic energy. Too far back =\n  unstable / can pop a wheel; this is a real tradeoff to model.\n- **Friction (the biggest lever after weight)** — Polished axles, removed burrs/crimp marks, and\n  dry graphite lubrication reduce rolling and bore friction. Less wheel-to-body and\n  wheel-to-axle-head contact = faster.\n- **Alignment** — A car that drifts into the rails or wanders loses energy. \"Rail-riding\" (a tiny\n  consistent steer) and reducing the number of wheels in contact reduce friction losses.\n- **Wheels** — Lighter, true (round, balanced) wheels with smooth bores have lower rotational\n  inertia and friction.\n- **Aerodynamics** — Minor at derby speeds but non-zero; lower, smoother bodies help slightly. Keep\n  this appropriately small so kids learn the *real* priority order (weight & friction first).\n\n> **Glossary**\n> - **PE/KE** — potential / kinetic energy; the core of why placement and weight matter.\n> - **Rail-riding** — deliberately steering a car to glide along one rail consistently.\n> - **DNF** — a car that derails or stalls; the sim should make failure modes legible.\n\n## Conventions\nRules the **developer** follows and the **auditor** checks:\n\n- **Architecture:** strict separation of a **pure simulation engine** (`pinewood_derby` package, no\n  UI / no I/O) from any UI layer (pygame, deferred). The engine is where the physics lives and\n  where TDD is richest.\n- **Engine style:** pure functions / immutable dataclasses, no hidden state, deterministic given\n  inputs (a fixed seed for any randomness) so tests are reproducible.\n- **Typing:** full type hints; **mypy `strict`** must pass. Model domain quantities explicitly\n  (don't pass bare floats where grams vs ounces could be confused — use named types/units).\n- **Testing:** `pytest`, behavior-driven against the engine's physics (e.g. \"more rear weight,\n  within the stable range, yields a faster time\"); test principles and tradeoffs, not\n  implementation details.\n- **Lint:** `ruff` clean.\n- **Dependencies:** keep the engine **dependency-free**; UI deps (pygame) may be added when the UI\n  is built.\n\n## Constraints\n- **Educational accuracy is a hard requirement** — simulated outcomes must reflect real derby\n  physics and the correct priority order of techniques. A \"fun but wrong\" result is a bug.\n- **Rich physics, simple presentation** — the simulation stays physically deep, but nothing\n  technical (Joules, Newtons, coefficients, integration) ever reaches the kid. Anything *shown* to a\n  ~12-year-old (or a group/demo) must be plain-language, visual, and immediately legible: \"where did\n  your speed go?\", not an energy ledger. Complexity lives behind the engine boundary; the\n  presentation layer translates it. A result a kid can't make sense of is a usability bug.\n- **No network, no accounts, no data collection** — safe for children; runs locally/offline.\n- **Deployment target:** local desktop (pygame) for v1; a browser front-end may be revisited later\n  (the pure engine ports either way).\n- **Accessibility:** age-appropriate, readable, keyboard-navigable UI when built.\n\n## Out of scope / known limitations\n- No real-hardware integration, no multiplayer, no user accounts/leaderboards (for now).\n- Not a CAD/3D modeling tool — it teaches principles, it doesn't design a physical cut file.\n- Aerodynamics is modeled at low fidelity by design.\n\n## Product-plane notes\n_(empty — the synthesizer will populate app-specific skills/hooks/learnings here as it discovers\nthem, e.g. a \"physics-validation\" skill if engine accuracy becomes a recurring friction point.)_\n","numLines":110,"startLine":1,"totalLines":110}}}
